  "summary": "The name is the art: 50,000-year-old handstyles updated in aerosol.",
  "description": "Graffiti writing — TAKI 183's 1971 New York Times fame canonized it — evolved from tag signatures to throw-ups to blockbuster whole-cars, inventing a complete technical culture: blackbooks, sketch exchanges, crew hierarchy, the crew-vs-crew \"beef\", and the ladder of fame (tags → throwies → pieces → productions). Its visual science: letter structure, color theory (3-color fade canons), wildstyle interlock, and the handstyle that graffiti writers consider the soul. Museums now collect what transit police sandblasted; Basquiat and Haring crossed over from the street; the culture's language (burner, king, toy, buff) spread worldwide while remaining earned-only — you can't buy your way in.",
  "culturalContext": "Graffiti writing turned the name into art: from TAKI 183 through Style Wars to gallery dual-careers, the culture invented a complete technical world — blackbooks, crews, handstyle as soul — and its language (burner, king, toy) spread globally while staying earned-only.",
